AWS Application Load Balancer Setup for Nginx Deployment
This guide outlines the complete steps to deploy an AWS Application Load Balancer (ALB) routing HTTP traffic on port 80 to an Nginx web server running on an EC2 instance.

Overview & Architecture
Region: us-east-1

EC2 Instance: datacenter-ec2

Load Balancer Name: datacenter-alb

Target Group Name: datacenter-tg

ALB Security Group Name: datacenter-sg (Inbound HTTP 80 from 0.0.0.0/0)

[ Internet ] 
     │
     ▼ (Port 80)
[ datacenter-sg ] ──► [ datacenter-alb ]
                             │
                             ▼ (Port 80 / Target Group: datacenter-tg)
                      [ EC2 Security Group ]
                             │
                             ▼
                      [ datacenter-ec2 ] (Nginx Server)
Prerequisites & Environment Setup
Configure your AWS CLI default region:

Bash
export AWS_DEFAULT_REGION="us-east-1"
aws sts get-caller-identity
Step-by-Step Implementation
1. Gather Existing AWS Resources
Retrieve the VPC ID, subnets, EC2 Instance ID, and EC2 Security Group ID:

Bash
# Get Default VPC ID
VPC_ID=$(aws ec2 describe-vpcs --filters "Name=isDefault,Values=true" --query "Vpcs[0].VpcId" --output text)

# Get Subnets associated with the VPC
SUBNETS=$(aws ec2 describe-subnets --filters "Name=vpc-id,Values=$VPC_ID" --query "Subnets[*].SubnetId" --output text)

# Get EC2 Instance ID and current Security Group ID for datacenter-ec2
EC2_ID=$(aws ec2 describe-instances --filters "Name=tag:Name,Values=datacenter-ec2" --query "Reservations[0].Instances[0].InstanceId" --output text)
EC2_SG=$(aws ec2 describe-instances --filters "Name=tag:Name,Values=datacenter-ec2" --query "Reservations[0].Instances[0].SecurityGroups[0].GroupId" --output text)
2. Create Security Group for ALB
Create datacenter-sg and open port 80 to the public (0.0.0.0/0):

Bash
# Create Security Group
ALB_SG=$(aws ec2 create-security-group \
  --group-name datacenter-sg \
  --description "Security group for datacenter ALB" \
  --vpc-id $VPC_ID \
  --query 'GroupId' --output text)

# Authorize ingress rule for Port 80
aws ec2 authorize-security-group-ingress \
  --group-id $ALB_SG \
  --protocol tcp \
  --port 80 \
  --cidr 0.0.0.0/0
3. Update EC2 Instance Security Group
Allow HTTP traffic on port 80 from the ALB security group (datacenter-sg) to the EC2 security group:

Bash
aws ec2 authorize-security-group-ingress \
  --group-id $EC2_SG \
  --protocol tcp \
  --port 80 \
  --source-group $ALB_SG
4. Create Target Group & Register Instance
Create the HTTP target group on port 80 and attach datacenter-ec2:

Bash
# Create Target Group
TG_ARN=$(aws elbv2 create-target-group \
  --name datacenter-tg \
  --protocol HTTP \
  --port 80 \
  --vpc-id $VPC_ID \
  --target-type instance \
  --query 'TargetGroups[0].TargetGroupArn' --output text)

# Register EC2 instance to Target Group
aws elbv2 register-targets \
  --target-group-arn $TG_ARN \
  --targets Id=$EC2_ID,Port=80
5. Create Application Load Balancer & Listener
Create datacenter-alb with the created subnets and security group, then attach a listener forwarding port 80 traffic to datacenter-tg:

Bash
# Create Application Load Balancer
ALB_ARN=$(aws elbv2 create-load-balancer \
  --name datacenter-alb \
  --subnets $SUBNETS \
  --security-groups $ALB_SG \
  --query 'LoadBalancers[0].LoadBalancerArn' --output text)

# Create Listener on Port 80
aws elbv2 create-listener \
  --load-balancer-arn $ALB_ARN \
  --protocol HTTP \
  --port 80 \
  --default-actions Type=forward,TargetGroupArn=$TG_ARN
Verification & Testing
Check Target Group Health
Verify that the target instance transitions to healthy:

Bash
aws elbv2 describe-target-health --target-group-arn $TG_ARN
Test ALB HTTP Request
Fetch the ALB DNS name and perform a curl request once provisioned:

Bash
ALB_DNS=$(aws elbv2 describe-load-balancers --load-balancer-arns $ALB_ARN --query "LoadBalancers[0].DNSName" --output text)

curl -I "http://$ALB_DNS"
